Added first-login profile handling and claims sync to the `[profile]` extra. The first time somebody authenticates, a Profile is minted for them in the `incomplete` state, seeded from the provider's claims and filed in the configured container. On every later login the provider's claims refresh the fields it still owns — and only those. Profile-owned fields are never clobbered, and rather than a flag per field the Profile simply remembers what the provider last wrote: the provider may write a field only while the current value still equals that. Editing a field ends the provider's claim on it, and so does clearing one, which is the case a flag-based design usually gets wrong — a value that reappears at the next login is indistinguishable from a bug. The login name is deliberately never synced: it is half of the case-folded index user enumeration queries, and a provider renaming somebody should not silently move their account. A user gets `Editor` on their own Profile and nothing on anybody else's, computed by a local-role provider rather than assigned at creation, so there is nothing to keep in step; `Editor` rather than `Owner`, because deleting your own Profile would break your account while your login kept working. `GET @my-profile` answers Volto's first-login routing question — where your Profile is and how far along it is — from the catalog, so the check every login performs costs no object load. All of it is driven purely by the documented event contract, and the end-to-end test proves it against a real Dex login rather than a fired event. @ericof
